From: Gilly1970 <gilroyscott1970@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7996: attribute radar events to the detecting band

mt7996_mcu_rx_radar_detected() picks the mt76_phy from rdd_idx, then calls
ieee80211_radar_detected(mphy->hw, NULL) - a NULL chanctx.

ieee80211_radar_detected() walks every chanctx of the wiphy and, because
ieee80211_radar_mark_chan_ctx_iterator() returns early only when
"data && data != chanctx_conf", a NULL data marks all of them.  All bands
share one wiphy, so a radar pulse seen by the 5 GHz or 6 GHz RDD also marks
the 2.4 GHz channel context as radar-affected, where no radar detector
exists at all.  The band then goes off-channel / CAC-holds for no reason,
and every BSS on it logs a radar event at its own frequency.

Pass the detecting phy's own channel context instead, falling back to NULL
(previous behaviour) if the phy has none at the time of the event.

Link: https://github.com/Gilly1970/Gemtek-W1700K-6.18
Signed-off-by: Gilly1970 <gilroyscott1970@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: airola <airola@example.org>
---
--- a/mt7996/mcu.c
+++ b/mt7996/mcu.c
@@ -562,10 +562,22 @@
 						&dev->rdd2_chandef,
 						GFP_ATOMIC);
 	} else {
+		struct ieee80211_chanctx_conf *conf = NULL;
 		struct mt7996_phy *phy = mphy->priv;
 
+		/* All bands share one wiphy: a radar reported with a NULL
+		 * chanctx marks every channel context, so each BSS logs a
+		 * radar event on its own frequency, including 2.4 GHz where
+		 * no radar detector exists. Report it on the detecting band
+		 * only.
+		 */
+		if (mphy->chanctx)
+			conf = container_of((void *)mphy->chanctx,
+					    struct ieee80211_chanctx_conf,
+					    drv_priv);
+
 		phy->rdd_tx_paused = true;
-		ieee80211_radar_detected(mphy->hw, NULL);
+		ieee80211_radar_detected(mphy->hw, conf);
 	}
 	dev->hw_pattern++;
 
